package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

// Matt: "i want a guide section about the blind eternities, and
// generally the "topology" of the mtg multiverse/universe/whatever".
internal val storyBlindEternities = StorySection(
    "blind-eternities", "The Blind Eternities",
    "Every world in Magic floats alone in a sea of raw chaos, and the shape of that sea decides who can travel, who can invade and what waits outside.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "the-multiverse", "The Multiverse",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "countless-planes", "Countless Planes",
                    listOf(
                        "Every story Magic has ever told happens somewhere in the Multiverse, the name for all of creation. It is not one world but more worlds than anyone has counted, and each of them is called a plane. A plane is a whole reality of its own, with its own land and sky, its own peoples and gods, and its own idea of how magic works. Some are a single round world much like ours, as Dominaria is. Ravnica is one city that has grown to cover its whole world. Zendikar is a wild place whose land heaves and shifts under the people trying to live on it. Most people born on a plane live and die without ever learning there is anything else.",
                        "What makes a plane matter, in the game and in the story, is mana. Every plane's land gives it off, and a mage draws on it by building a bond with places they know. A forest on Dominaria and a forest on Zendikar both feed green magic, but each feels of its own world, which is why the same five basic lands look different in every set. Mana is also what makes a plane worth taking. The Phyrexians wanted planes to convert. The [Eldrazi](the-binding) wanted planes to eat. [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) wanted every plane's power gathered into himself.",
                        "Between the planes there is nothing ordinary at all. There is no space to fly across and no sky to sail. There is only the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities), the chaos every plane floats in, and almost everything strange about the Multiverse's map comes from how hard that chaos is to cross.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dominia", "Dominia",
                    listOf(
                        "For much of Magic's history the story barely needed a word for the Multiverse, because it hardly ever left one plane. The early novels called all of creation Dominia, and they treated Dominaria as its heart, the plane more threads ran through than any other. It was certainly the plane where the most happened. [Urza](who/urza) and Mishra tore it apart in the [Brothers' War](the-war). The Phyrexians came for it again and again. Its own time was broken by the [rifts](time-rifts) that nearly unmade it.",
                        "The planes near it in those stories were close and well travelled. Phyrexia was reached from Dominaria through a portal the Thran opened under [Halcyon](halcyon), and [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) made that plane his own. Rath was built in a pocket of reality attached to Dominaria itself. Mercadia, Serra's Realm and Ulgrotha all lay within reach of a planeswalker of that age, and the planeswalkers of that age were nearly gods. The old cosmology was small and crowded, and a surprising amount of it was built by hand.",
                        "Over time the story began to speak of the Multiverse instead, and it grew. After the [Mending](the-mending) new planes were visited one after another, Alara, Zendikar, Innistrad and many more, each with its own rules and its own kind of trouble, and Dominaria became one world among a great many. It is still the plane the story keeps coming home to.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "worlds-within-worlds", "Worlds Within Worlds",
                    listOf(
                        "A plane does not have to be one world. Theros is the mortal world, and also Nyx, the starry realm of its gods, and also the Underworld beneath them both, and {Nykthos, Shrine to Nyx} is a temple at the border. Kamigawa is a material world laid against a spirit world, and the two once went to war. Kaldheim is ten realms hanging on the branches of {The World Tree}, with the dark Cosmos between them, and only the [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths), a god's strength or a longboat carved with runes could carry anyone across. Phyrexia was built as spheres nested one inside the next, as [Yawgmoth's Spheres](yawgmoths-spheres) tells.",
                        "Some planes are hollow. Mirrodin began as Argentum, a sphere of metal that [Karn](who/karn) built around an empty heart, told in [Argentum](argentum). Ixalan turned out to have a whole world inside it, the Core, lit by a sun of its own. Some planes are broken. Alara was once a single world before it was split into five shards, each keeping three colours of mana and losing the other two, until Nicol Bolas drew them back together, told in [Alara](alara).",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-edge", "The Edge",
                    listOf(
                        "Nobody has ever found an outside to the Multiverse, but in Edge of Eternities the story found an edge. Beyond every world the planeswalkers knew lies the Edge, a single plane of billions of stars wrapped like a ring around the rest of creation. Its people call it simply space. They travel between its stars on eternity columns and torch ships, and looking inward their instruments end at a scrambled barrier they call the Chaos Wall.",
                        "What lies behind the Chaos Wall is everything else, every plane in this Guide and the Blind Eternities between them, and the people of the Edge had no idea. Then Tezzeret came out of the void and made himself one of the most feared criminals around the dying star Sothera, which is told in [Edge of Eternities](edge-of-eternities). It is the strangest corner of the map so far, a plane that is a whole universe of its own, sitting at the rim of all the others.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Dominaria"),
                StorySource("Set", "Theros Beyond Death"),
                StorySource("Set", "Kaldheim"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Lost Caverns of Ixalan"),
                StorySource("Set", "Shards of Alara"),
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Multiverse, Plane, Dominia"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "the-blind-eternities", "The Blind Eternities",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-void-between", "The Void Between",
                    listOf(
                        "Between the planes lies the Blind Eternities. It is not space and it is not empty. It is chaos without any shape at all, with no up or down, no light to see by, and no time in any sense a mortal would recognise. The planes drift in it like islands in a storm, and everything that is not a plane is part of it. Nothing born on a plane can survive it unprotected. Anyone who falls into it without a spark is simply undone.",
                        "A planeswalker can cross it because the [spark](crossing-the-void) protects them. They do not sail or fly. They walk with their will, holding the world they want in mind and pulling themselves toward it through the chaos, and the walk can be quick or long and hard. Many planeswalkers made their first walk without meaning to, thrown out of their home world the moment their spark ignited, and arrived somewhere they had never heard of with no idea how they had come there.",
                        "Because nothing could cross it but a planeswalker, most planes spent their whole history alone, never knowing any other world existed. That isolation is the real shape of the Multiverse. The planes only ever touched where something carried them across the void, a planeswalker, a portal, a bridge or, much later, an [Omenpath](the-omenpaths).",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "raw-aether", "Raw Aether",
                    listOf(
                        "What the Blind Eternities are made of is usually called aether. It is the raw stuff of creation before it has become anything, unformed and always moving, and some stories describe the planes as what that stuff becomes once it settles into shape. When [Karn](who/karn) wanted a world of his own, he found an empty stretch between the planes and made Argentum out of its raw substance.",
                        "Some of the aether finds its way into the planes. On Kaladesh it fills the sky in bright currents, and the plane's inventors catch it, refine it and burn it to run their machines, as {Aether Hub} shows. Planechase gave the chaos a card of its own, {Chaotic Aether}, a moment when the void breaks into a game of Magic and nothing behaves as it should.",
                        "The void can be wounded too. At the end of the invasion of the Multiverse, Elspeth Tirel carried {The Filigree Sylex} into the Blind Eternities and set it off there. [Teferi](who/teferi) came to suspect that the wound it left, together with the swap of Zhalfir for New Phyrexia, is why the sparks of the planeswalkers went out afterward, told in [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost). Nobody has proved it.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "what-lives-outside", "What Lives Outside",
                    listOf(
                        "Some things are at home in the chaos. The Eldrazi are the most famous of them. What appears on a plane as {Ulamog, the Ceaseless Hunger} or {Emrakul, the Aeons Torn} is only the part of a titan that reaches into the world, the way a hand reaches into a pond and the fish see only the hand. Their true forms are in the Blind Eternities, larger than any plane, and they drift from world to world feeding on mana and life until there is nothing left but dust. [Ugin](who/ugin) studied them for ages and never claimed to understand them.",
                        "Long ago Ugin, [Sorin](who/sorin) and [Nahiri](who/nahiri) lured three titans to Zendikar and bound them there in a lattice of hedrons, told in [The Binding](the-binding). Held in a single world, the titans could neither feed nor leave. When they were freed at last, Ulamog and Kozilek were destroyed on Zendikar, and Emrakul was [sealed away](emrakul-on-innistrad) again inside Innistrad's silver moon. What their deaths on one plane meant for whatever was left of them out in the void, the story has never said.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Rise of the Eldrazi"),
                StorySource("Set", "Battle for Zendikar"),
                StorySource("Set", "Kaladesh"),
                StorySource("Set", "Planechase 2012"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Blind Eternities, Aether, Eldrazi"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "crossing-the-void", "Crossing the Void",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-spark", "The Spark",
                    listOf(
                        "A planeswalker is somebody born with a spark, a rare power that lets its bearer leave their plane and cross the Blind Eternities to another. Most people who carry one never learn it is there. A spark ignites in a moment of great stress, often pain or terror or the edge of death, and the person is torn out of their world and flung across the void. [Urza](who/urza) found his when the Golgothian Sylex blew at the end of the [Brothers' War](sylex-blast), and walked away from the ruin of Dominaria into a Multiverse he had never imagined.",
                        "The planeswalkers before the [Mending](the-mending), called the [Oldwalkers](oldwalkers) now, were close to gods. They did not age, they could take any shape they liked, they could carry others with them and they could make whole planes out of nothing. When the time rifts tore at the Multiverse, [Teferi](who/teferi) gave up his spark to close one and [Karn](who/karn) lost his closing another, and when the last was sealed the spark itself had been rewritten.",
                        "Planeswalkers after the Mending are mortal. They age and bleed and need to eat, they can take little more than the clothes they wear, and they cannot carry anyone with them. They cannot build worlds. They can still walk between them, and for a long stretch of the story that made them the only people in the Multiverse who could.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "bridges-and-portals", "Bridges and Portals",
                    listOf(
                        "Without a spark the only way across is to build one. The first crossing the story remembers was a portal the Thran opened under [Halcyon](halcyon), which carried [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) and his followers to the plane he would make into Phyrexia. Rebuilt Thran portals later let Phyrexia reach other worlds, and a portal in the caves of [Koilos](koilos) let its spies into Dominaria during the Brothers' War. In [Invasion](invasion) the portals opened all over Dominaria at once. {Phyrexian Portal} and {Portal to Phyrexia} show that road from both ends.",
                        "After the Mending every device ever built to cross between worlds stopped working, and the crossings had to be invented again. On Kaladesh, Tezzeret, working for [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), built a {Planar Bridge} out of the stolen work of the plane's inventors, and later wore its power in his own arm, which is why War of the Spark printed him as {Tezzeret, Master of the Bridge}. On Ravnica, Bolas lit the {Interplanar Beacon} to call planeswalkers to him, and the Immortal Sun stolen from Ixalan held every one of them there once they had come, told in [War of the Spark](ravnica-war-of-the-spark).",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "every-door-open", "Every Door Open",
                    listOf(
                        "Then the Phyrexians built the biggest bridge of all. Realmbreaker drove its branches through the Blind Eternities and pinned plane after plane for the [invasion of the Multiverse](invasion-of-the-multiverse). When the invasion was beaten and Wrenn turned the tree, the passages its branches had punched through the void did not close. They took the name of the [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths), after the passages that had once joined the realms of Kaldheim. Through them anybody at all can walk from one plane to another, without a spark and without a machine, and {Kellan, Planar Trailblazer} is one of the first to make a life of it.",
                        "In the same weeks the sparks went out. Almost every planeswalker lost theirs, quietly, told in [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost). So the shape of the Multiverse turned inside out. For all of Magic's history the planes were islands that only a rare few could leave. Now they are joined by roads that anyone can take, and hardly anyone can cross the old way. Ravnica's Firemind studies them, Avishkar has sent embassies down them, and a demon has learned to disguise his own doors as theirs.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Time Spiral, Planar Chaos, Future Sight"),
                StorySource("Set", "Aether Revolt"),
                StorySource("Set", "War of the Spark"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Planeswalker, Planar bridge, Omenpath"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "made-and-unmade", "Planes Made and Unmade",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "built-by-hand", "Built by Hand",
                    listOf(
                        "Before the Mending the strongest planeswalkers could make worlds. Serra, a planeswalker who wanted a refuge from war, made Serra's Realm, a plane of clouds and light filled with angels, and {Serra's Sanctum} is the land that remembers it. [Karn](who/karn) made Argentum, a silver sphere of a world, from the raw stuff between the planes, and it became Mirrodin, told in [Argentum](argentum). [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) had Rath made in a pocket of reality attached to Dominaria, a plane of flowstone grown on purpose, told in [Rath](rath-made).",
                        "A made plane is a different kind of thing from a born one. It is smaller, it has edges, and it does what its maker built it for. Rath's flowstone held its boundaries apart and added mass until it matched Dominaria, so that it could be laid over the older world and set down a Phyrexian army in a single moment, which is what it did in [Invasion](invasion). Mirrodin was meant to be a quiet place, and the drop of Phyrexian oil Karn carried into it turned it into New Phyrexia, told in [Scars](scars). Since the Mending no planeswalker has been able to make a plane at all.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "moved-and-merged", "Moved and Merged",
                    listOf(
                        "Planes can move. When the Phyrexians invaded Dominaria, [Teferi](who/teferi) phased his homeland of Zhalfir out of the world to keep it safe, and it was meant to come back when the danger had passed. It never quite did. When the rift at Zhalfir was sealed at the wrong moment during the [Mending](the-mending), the phased lands were set adrift in the Multiverse as a pocket plane of their own. Long afterward, in the last hours of the invasion of the Multiverse, Wrenn and the tree she called Eight swapped the places of Zhalfir and New Phyrexia, and Zhalfir came back into the world.",
                        "Planes can also come apart and come back together. Alara was split into five shards long ago, each with three colours of mana and none of the others, and the shards forgot they had ever been one world. When they began to merge again in the Conflux, it was because [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) had arranged it, to harvest the surge of mana released as a plane healed itself, told in [Alara](alara). Planechase plays with the same idea. {Spatial Merging} lays two planes on top of each other, and {Interplanar Tunnel} opens a way to whichever comes next.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "devoured", "Devoured",
                    listOf(
                        "Planes can die. The Eldrazi leave nothing behind them but dust, and the first thing [Sorin](who/sorin) and [Nahiri](who/nahiri) ever saw of Ulamog was a world being eaten, told in [The Binding](the-binding). Zendikar itself survived its titans only because they were bound in it rather than free, and then because its people and its planeswalkers fought until two of the three were destroyed.",
                        "Phyrexia unmade worlds another way. It did not eat them, it converted them. Mirrodin went from a metal plane full of life to New Phyrexia, every sphere of it compleated, and the [praetors](praetors) meant to do the same to every world they could reach. [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) built the invasion so that it would end with every plane joined to hers by Realmbreaker's branches, one great Phyrexia instead of many worlds. {Elesh Norn, Mother of Machines} shows her at the height of that plan. It failed, and the branches pulled back.",
                        "So the map is never finished. Planes are made, moved, merged, eaten and converted, and the Blind Eternities hold all of them, the one sea every world is an island in.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Urza's Saga"),
                StorySource("Set", "Tempest"),
                StorySource("Set", "Scars of Mirrodin"),
                StorySource("Set", "Conflux"),
                StorySource("Set", "Planechase 2012"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Artificial plane, Zhalfir, Alara, Rath"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
